WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.909
Film Courage: So Write Your Life, is that
part of The Travis Technique?

00:00:02.909 --> 00:00:03.909
Mark W. Travis: Yes.

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:04.909
It’s a part.

00:00:04.909 --> 00:00:07.980
There are 3 main elements of The Travis Technique.
1) is Write Your Life which is the autobiographical

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:08.980
storytelling.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:12.099
Which is, besides telling your own personal
stories, it’s all the basics of storytelling,

00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:13.099
all the structural aspects of telling any
kind of story.

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:14.650
So it really grounds you in storytelling.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:16.550
And one thing that’s important is it’s
not a writing class, per se.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:17.550
It’s storytelling.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.550
Can you tell the story?

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:22.800
Directors, writers and actors need to learn
how to tell the stories not just write them.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.050
Writing comes next.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:29.080
Can I actually sit down and tell you a story,
take 3 minutes and tell you a story?

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.280
And then can I examine my own character within
the story (know my own character)?

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.630
And can I consciously take you on a journey
through that little 3-minute story?

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:37.280
When you tell an autobiographical story, the
goal is…if I were to tell you an autobiographical

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.420
story right now Karen, my goal would be, I’m
going to take you through an event, and it

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:43.380
will take me 3-5 minutes to tell you, and
my goal is to have you experience while you’re

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.540
listening to the story exactly what I experienced
when I lived it without having to tell you

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.840
“Well then at that point I was really sad
and all that.”

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.480
I don’t have to fill that in.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.536
I will tell you the story in such a way that
you get it immediately.

00:00:55.536 --> 00:00:56.536
Now that’s the power of storytelling whether
it’s verbal or cinema or a play or even

00:00:56.536 --> 00:00:57.536
a novel (to be able to do this).

00:00:57.536 --> 00:00:58.536
So that’s what Write Your Life is about.

00:00:58.536 --> 00:00:59.536
It’s a very powerful experience.

00:00:59.536 --> 00:01:00.536
This is why a lot of people take it over and
over and over again.

00:01:00.536 --> 00:01:01.536
They keep coming back and take it again.

00:01:01.536 --> 00:01:02.536
Take it again.

00:01:02.536 --> 00:01:03.536
It’s like going back to the gym and working
out all those muscle of telling it again,

00:01:03.536 --> 00:01:04.536
the basics.

00:01:04.536 --> 00:01:05.536
The other 2 aspects of The Travis Technique
which is the Interrogation Process (which

00:01:05.536 --> 00:01:06.550
you know about a little bit).

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.890
The directorial technique of working with
actors to access the character.

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:15.650
It’s a process of not directing the actors,
but actually accessing the characters, talking

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:20.780
to the characters and how to stimulate the
characters with the understanding that when

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:26.740
you cast somebody in a movie, basically from
my point of view when I cast somebody, I am

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.010
communicating to that actor “The character
I want you to play exists already.”

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:31.530
I tell the actors this.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.690
The character exists already inside of you.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:34.690
You do not need to create the character.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:38.980
You do not need to develop the character,
you do not need to do anything.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:42.670
One thing that you have to do which is very,
very hard (it’s almost impossible), you

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:46.870
have to get out of the way and let the character
come out because the character is inside of

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:47.870
you.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.970
The problem is, you’re in the way.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:52.860
Because the character is inside you.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:53.990
The problem is, you’re in the way but I
can help you.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:55.270
Now that’s the Interrogation Process which
helps the actor actually remove themselves

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:56.910
from a process and allow the character to
emerge and we can talk more about that.

00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:00.299
The third part of The Travis Technique is
the power of staging.

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:07.110
And this all has to do with very simple concept
that is so powerful that we all deal with

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.060
every moment of our lives.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.060
Which is we move in the world, pretty much
in the same way (all of us), we all move around

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.060
the world avoiding conflicts, seeking comfort,
even as we set up to shoot this [video], that’s

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.060
what we did.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.060
We all move in relationship to the environment,
and to other people and even the way we sit,

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.060
what chair we want, we all move this way.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.060
Now this is how we move in life in response
to all the elements around us.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.060
The Power of Staging is we’re creating something
artificial.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:17.060
So I can stage a scene in such a way (which
I can demonstrate)…I can stage a scene in

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.060
such a way that how I move the actors, in
relationship to each other, in relationship

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.060
to the environment (whatever it is) …and
even in terms of their own body language,

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.060
I can move them and actually stimulate within
every actor the emotion I need for the character

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.060
at that moment.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.060
Whether it’s comfort, discomfort, anger,
rage, abandonment, whatever it is.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.720
I can move them in such a way that this gets
triggered in the actor.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.730
And the actor does not have to create that
feeling.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:30.069
The actor has to now struggle with that feeling.

00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:31.069
As we move through life (all of us), we’re
impacted and we have emotional reactions to

00:02:31.069 --> 00:02:32.069
things.

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:33.069
We feel sad, we feel happy, we feel abandoned,
we feel uncomfortable, whatever.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:34.069
We have emotional reactions.

00:02:34.069 --> 00:02:35.069
We don’t go through life trying to create
emotion.

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:36.069
I don’t know of anybody who has ever done
that.

00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:37.069
“I think I am going to be happy in the next
second and I’m going to…”

00:02:37.069 --> 00:02:38.069
No.

00:02:38.069 --> 00:02:39.069
We don’t do that.

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:40.069
We would like to have that but we have these
emotional reactions.

00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:41.069
So my goal which is very successful in working
with staging is I trigger emotions inside

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:42.069
the actor which are appropriate for the character
and now as the character they have to struggle

00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:43.069
with that emotion.

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:44.069
What to do with it.

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:45.069
And now it’s authentic.

00:02:45.069 --> 00:02:46.069
You’re not watching an actor create an emotion
or pretend to have an emotion.

00:02:46.069 --> 00:02:47.069
You’re watching an actual person struggling
with or embracing (whatever they’re doing),

00:02:47.069 --> 00:02:48.069
dealing with genuine emotions.

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:49.069
And that’s the Power of Staging.

00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:50.069
And the other aspect of that is when it’s
working well, the audience sees it immediately.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:51.069
The audience will react emotionally, too.

00:02:51.069 --> 00:02:52.069
You are actually triggering emotions in the
audience simultaneously as I’m doing it

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:53.069
with the actor.

00:02:53.069 --> 00:02:54.069
So again, getting back to what we were talking
about before, because of projection.

00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:55.069
Because when we watch a scene or we watch
a movie or we watch a play, anything.

00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:56.069
Even if we watch people in life we project
ourselves into them.

00:02:56.069 --> 00:02:57.069
And we see two people arguing at another table
and we say “Ohhhh…he’s so uncomfortable!”

00:02:57.069 --> 00:02:58.069
Well, why are you reacting that way?

00:02:58.069 --> 00:02:59.069
Because you’re saying “If I was there,
this is how I would be feeling.”

00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:00.069
So through staging, I can trigger emotions
in the audience.

00:03:00.069 --> 00:03:01.069
And help the audience identify more deeply
with the character.

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:02.069
So those are the three elements.

00:03:02.069 --> 00:03:03.069
Film Courage: So when you’re doing a weekend
seminar, are you beginning with the writing

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:04.069
portion and then working in Interrogation
and then doing the Staging or no?

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:05.069
Mark W. Travis: Well, not in a weekend.

00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:06.069
[Laughs] It’s a great question and it’s
something that Elsha and I struggle with all

00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:07.069
the time for every workshop we put together.

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:08.069
Our ideal situation, yes we would deal with
all of it.

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:09.069
But then it’s going to take days or weeks
because each one is so massive.

00:03:09.069 --> 00:03:10.069
So what we have is a weekend job that we do
frequently that is just called Write Your

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:11.069
Life and you can learn that.

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:12.069
Then we have other weekend workshops that
we do which are longer which are in the Interrogation

00:03:12.069 --> 00:03:13.069
process.

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:14.069
The last time we did that it was actually
longer, it was 10 days.

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:15.069
Just for directors, just to learn the Interrogation
process.

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:16.069
Or we will include the Interrogation Process
and staging together, so The Travis Technique

00:03:16.069 --> 00:03:17.069
is really an umbrella of a lot of stuff.

00:03:17.069 --> 00:03:18.069
And we have found if we try to teach too much
in a short period of time, we’re skimming

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:19.069
over the surface and we like to do workshops
where you can really plunge yourself into

00:03:19.069 --> 00:03:20.069
one technique, one aspect of it to really
benefit from digging that deep into the process.

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:21.069
Film Courage: One last question and then we’ll
move on, in terms of the Writing Your Life

00:03:21.069 --> 00:03:22.069
portion, what are some thing you see get in
the way of people telling an authentic scene

00:03:22.069 --> 00:03:23.069
from a version of their life?

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.069
Do people tend to paint too rosy of a picture
of a scene that is really from their own life?

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:25.069
Or the opposite (because people tend to embellish
memories that aren’t there) that are too

00:03:25.069 --> 00:03:26.069
traumatic and you can kind of sense that it’s
not authentic.

00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:27.069
Mark W. Travis: Well, yes, all of that can
happen and there is more that can happen.

00:03:27.069 --> 00:03:28.069
First of all we have to look at someone…I
mean next time (wherever you are)…if you

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:29.069
and David are some place and your friends
tell a story, just listen and ask yourself

00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:30.069
(seriously) and this is not critical at all,
why are they telling this story?

00:03:30.069 --> 00:03:31.069
“Why is she telling this story?

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:32.069
Why is he telling this story?”

00:03:32.069 --> 00:03:33.069
You know, there is an agenda, there is a reason,
maybe they are telling this story to make

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:34.069
a point clearer that they were trying to make
earlier and they are using the story to tell

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:35.069
it.

00:03:35.069 --> 00:03:36.069
Fine.

00:03:36.069 --> 00:03:37.069
Maybe they are telling the story for self-aggrandizement?

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:38.069
Maybe they are telling because they want sympathy
or pity?

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:39.069
Who knows?

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:40.069
There is always a reason for telling the story.

00:03:40.069 --> 00:03:41.069
And since it’s an autobiographical story,
the reason to tell the story is for you.

00:03:41.069 --> 00:03:42.069
“I want to tell the story so you understand
something about me.”

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:43.069
So I’m doing it for me.

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:44.069
I’m trying to elicit something out of you.

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:45.069
Well what we do in Write Your Life is can
you tell your story and think of the story

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:46.069
as a gift?

00:03:46.069 --> 00:03:47.069
I want to give you a gift.

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:48.069
The gift is the experience you are going to
go through hearing this story.

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:49.069
It has nothing to do with me, even though
it’s my story.

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:50.069
Because when we make a film or tell a story,
that’s a gift to the world.

00:03:50.069 --> 00:03:51.069
If I make a movie, I don’t want people to
be thinking of Me The Director.

00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:52.069
Not at all.

00:03:52.069 --> 00:03:53.069
I want them involved in the story.

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:54.069
And the gift of the story, whether it’s
told or it’s a film, the gift of the story

00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:55.069
is two-fold. 1) is what the viewer or listener
is experiencing as the story is unfolding.

00:03:55.069 --> 00:03:56.069
That is one gift.

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:57.069
Those 2 hours of a movie or 5 minutes of listening
to the story.

00:03:57.069 --> 00:03:58.069
That’s one gift.

00:03:58.069 --> 00:03:59.069
The other gift, which is even bigger in a
way, is the legacy of the story.

00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:00.069
We’ve all seen movies that we will never
forget.

00:04:00.069 --> 00:04:01.069
We’ve all seen movies that have changed
our lives in a certain way and sometimes we’re

00:04:01.069 --> 00:04:02.069
not even aware of it until years later and
we go “Wow!

00:04:02.069 --> 00:04:03.069
When I saw that movie…something shifted!”

00:04:03.069 --> 00:04:04.069
That is the power of what we’re doing.

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:05.069
That’s also the gift of storytelling that
it can last forever.

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:06.069
And again, it’s not about the storyteller.

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:07.069
So getting back to your question, one of the
traps you can fall into, are you’re telling

00:04:07.069 --> 00:04:08.069
the story so that the listener will see you
in a different way?

00:04:08.069 --> 00:04:09.069
Well then it’s all about you.

00:04:09.069 --> 00:04:10.069
Or you doing it for self-aggrandizement or
something else?

00:04:10.069 --> 00:04:11.069
Are you willing…this is a big question in
Write Your Life…are you willing to tell

00:04:11.069 --> 00:04:12.069
the story and reveal (really) how vulnerable
you were?

00:04:12.069 --> 00:04:13.069
How stupid you were?

00:04:13.069 --> 00:04:14.069
How arrogant you were?

00:04:14.069 --> 00:04:15.069
Which means how human you were at that time?

00:04:15.069 --> 00:04:16.069
Can you do that?

00:04:16.069 --> 00:04:17.069
That’s not asking for pity.

00:04:17.069 --> 00:04:18.069
In other words, how honest can you be?

00:04:18.069 --> 00:04:19.069
So what we focus on in the Write Your Life
is can you tell it honestly?

00:04:19.069 --> 00:04:20.069
Get the agenda out of the way!

00:04:20.069 --> 00:04:21.069
Your agenda now is to be honest.

00:04:21.069 --> 00:04:22.069
That is what a lot of people struggle with.

00:04:22.069 --> 00:04:23.069
They struggle first of all with looking at
what was really going on and when they see

00:04:23.069 --> 00:04:24.069
it, it gets a little shaky.

00:04:24.069 --> 00:04:25.069
Then to reveal it gets even shakier.

00:04:25.069 --> 00:04:26.069
That’s the business we’re in.

00:04:26.069 --> 00:04:27.069
We’re storytellers and we don’t tell stories
for people to change their opinion about us.

00:04:27.069 --> 00:04:28.069
We actually tell stories so people can have
an experience.

00:04:28.069 --> 00:04:29.069
That may shift something within them, maybe
even change their opinion about themselves.

00:04:29.069 --> 00:04:30.069
It’s not about us.

00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:31.069
We have to tell these stories honestly, authentically.

00:04:31.069 --> 00:04:32.069
We, the director, writer and actors actually
have to (this is really ironic) we are the

00:04:32.069 --> 00:04:33.069
primary…this is what I call the Golden Triangle
(Director, actors and writer - That’s The

00:04:33.069 --> 00:04:34.069
Golden Triangle.

00:04:34.069 --> 00:04:35.069
The rest is the support system.)

00:04:35.069 --> 00:04:36.069
We as that Golden Triangle, the primary triangle
of telling stories, have to get out of the

00:04:36.069 --> 00:04:37.069
way.

00:04:37.069 --> 00:04:38.069
We have to remove ourselves from the very
thing we’re doing, which is very hard.

00:04:38.069 --> 00:04:39.069
It’s not about the writer.

00:04:39.069 --> 00:04:40.069
It’s not about the director.

00:04:40.069 --> 00:04:41.069
And it’s certainly not about the actors.

00:04:41.069 --> 00:04:42.069
It’s about the characters.

00:04:42.069 --> 00:04:43.069
We have to get out of the way and serve the
story and serve the characters and that is

00:04:43.069 --> 00:04:44.069
hard.

00:04:44.069 --> 00:04:45.069
Question for the Viewers: Please share your
thoughts.

00:04:45.069 --> 00:04:45.083
What do you think about what Mark W. Travis
is saying in this video?

